[THOUGHT] No infectious exposures identified — this makes infectious colitis less likely, though not impossible (e.g., community-acquired C. difficile or norovirus). With recent MI, diabetes, and hypertension, mesenteric ischemia remains high on the differential. Next, domain #7 — targeted physical examination — is essential. Key signs include abdominal tenderness (location, rebound, guarding), bowel sounds (hypoactive in ischemia), fever (absent so far), and peripheral perfusion (e.g., pulses, capillary refill) to assess for ongoing cardiovascular compromise. Since I cannot perform exam directly, I must request objective findings.

[TEST] Please perform and report:  
- Abdominal examination: location and character of tenderness (e.g., periumbilical, RLQ, diffuse), presence of rebound tenderness or involuntary guarding, and bowel sounds (present/absent/hyperactive);  
- Vital signs: temperature, heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation;  
- Peripheral perfusion: capillary refill time and radial/femoral pulse quality.

[THOUGHT] The epigastric bruit is a critical finding — highly suggestive of mesenteric artery stenosis or turbulent flow, often in the setting of atherosclerosis (consistent with long-standing diabetes, HTN, and recent MI). Combined with hypotension (BP 95/65), tachycardia (HR 95), periumbilical tenderness, and bloody diarrhea, this strongly supports acute mesenteric ischemia — particularly chronic mesenteric ischemia with acute-on-chronic decompensation or early acute mesenteric arterial occlusion. Bowel sounds and rebound/guarding are missing but urgently needed: absent/hypoactive bowel sounds would further support ischemia; rebound/guarding could indicate peritoneal irritation (worsening ischemia or infarction). Also, oxygen saturation and capillary refill are essential to assess end-organ perfusion.

[TEST] Please perform and report:  
- Abdominal examination: presence/absence and quality of bowel sounds (e.g., absent, hypoactive, normoactive, hyperactive); presence or absence of rebound tenderness and involuntary guarding;  
- Peripheral perfusion: capillary refill time (seconds) and radial pulse quality (e.g., strong, weak, absent);  
- Oxygen saturation (SpO₂) on room air.

